{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The cubital fossa is a triangular hollow on the front of the elbow. Its base is an imaginary line between the humeral epicondyles, its lateral border is brachioradialis and its medial border is pronator teres. From medial to lateral it contains the median nerve, brachial artery, biceps tendon and radial nerve. The median cubital vein is in its roof.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN11.5, AN11.3" phase="Phase 1" /%}

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/cubital-fossa/cubital-fossa-overview-labelled.webp" alt="Labelled diagram of the right cubital fossa showing its muscular borders and the median nerve, brachial artery, biceps tendon and radial nerve inside it." caption="The cubital fossa is a triangular hollow at the front of the elbow, and its contents lie in a fixed order from medial to lateral." credit="Maen K. Househ, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cubital_Fossa2.png" width="960" height="493" /%}

## What is the cubital fossa?

The word cubital comes from the Latin *cubitus*, meaning elbow. The cubital fossa is a **triangular hollow** on the front of the elbow. It is the area where the arm joins the forearm, and it matters because it carries several major structures between the two.

It is **homologous** with the popliteal fossa of the lower limb. Homologous means that the two have the same basic plan. The popliteal fossa lies on the back of the knee.

The fossa is actually very narrow. The contents are clearly seen only after the boundaries are pulled apart in dissection.

## Boundaries

{% table %}
- **Part**
- **Formed by**
---
- **Base (above)**
- An imaginary line joining the front of the two epicondyles of the humerus
---
- **Lateral border**
- Brachioradialis (its medial border)
---
- **Medial border**
- Pronator teres (its lateral border)
---
- **Apex (below)**
- Directed downwards. It is formed where brachioradialis crosses pronator teres.
---
- **Roof**
- Skin, superficial fascia, deep fascia and the bicipital aponeurosis
---
- **Floor**
- Brachialis medially and supinator laterally
{% /table %}

## Roof and floor in detail

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/cubital-fossa/bicipital-aponeurosis-roof-floor.webp" alt="Anterior view of the elbow showing the biceps tendon, the bicipital aponeurosis crossing the forearm flexors, and the brachialis and supinator muscles in the floor." caption="The bicipital aponeurosis crosses the fossa in the roof, while brachialis and supinator form the floor." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sobo_1909_274.png" width="960" height="2216" /%}

**Roof**, from superficial to deep:

- **Skin.**
- **Superficial fascia.** This contains the veins that join across the front of the elbow: the cephalic, median cubital and basilic veins, from lateral to medial. The lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm runs with the cephalic vein. The medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm runs with the basilic vein.
- **Deep fascia.** It is continuous with the deep fascia of the front of the arm and is attached to the medial and lateral humeral epicondyles.
- **Bicipital aponeurosis.** This is a fibrous sheet that strengthens the deep fascia. It sweeps across the medial and lower part of the fossa to the fascia over the superficial flexor muscles.

**Floor:** brachialis and supinator. Supinator surrounds the upper part of the radius.

## Contents from medial to lateral

The tendon of biceps brachii runs down the middle and divides the fossa into a medial and a lateral part. The order from medial to lateral is:

{% table %}
- **Structure**
- **Key points in the fossa**
---
- **Median nerve**
- Lies on the medial side of the brachial artery. It gives branches to flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus and flexor digitorum superficialis. It leaves the fossa by passing between the two heads of pronator teres.
---
- **Brachial artery (end of it)**
- It enters the fossa close to the midline and divides in the lower part of the fossa into the **radial and ulnar arteries**, each with accompanying veins. The bicipital aponeurosis lies in front of it.
---
- **Tendon of biceps brachii**
- Lies between the brachial artery and the radial nerve
---
- **Radial nerve**
- Lies on a deeper plane, on the lateral side of the biceps tendon. It divides here into a deep branch and a superficial branch.
{% /table %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Order, medial to lateral, MBBR:** **M**edian nerve, **B**rachial artery, tendon of **B**iceps, **R**adial nerve.
{% /callout %}

## The brachial artery and its branches at the fossa

The brachial artery is the continuation of the axillary artery. It begins at the lower border of the tendon of teres major. At first it lies medial to the humerus, then it spirals forward until it lies midway between the humeral epicondyles. It ends about a centimetre below the elbow joint, at the level of the neck of the radius, by dividing into the radial and ulnar arteries.

It is wholly superficial and is covered in front only by skin and fasciae. At the elbow the **bicipital aponeurosis crosses in front of it** and separates it from the median cubital vein. It is accompanied by two venae comitantes (companion veins that run on each side of an artery).

Branches of the terminal arteries seen in the fossa:

- **Radial artery:** smaller and more superficial than the ulnar artery. It gives off the radial recurrent branch.
- **Ulnar artery:** passes deep to both heads of pronator teres. It gives off the anterior ulnar recurrent, the posterior ulnar recurrent and the common interosseous artery. The common interosseous divides into the anterior and posterior interosseous arteries. The posterior interosseous gives off the interosseous recurrent branch.

## The radial nerve in the fossa

The radial nerve descends in front of the lateral epicondyle to enter the fossa. Here it gives off the **posterior interosseous nerve** (the deep branch). This nerve supplies extensor carpi radialis brevis and supinator, then leaves the fossa by piercing supinator. The remaining **superficial branch** runs down the front of the forearm for some distance.

## Veins of the cubital region

The superficial veins lie in the roof of the fossa.

- The **cephalic vein** comes up the lateral side of the forearm.
- The **basilic vein** lies on the medial side.
- The **median cubital vein** joins the two. It is a large communicating vein that shunts blood from the cephalic to the basilic vein. It begins about 2.5 cm below the bend of the elbow, runs upwards and medially over the bicipital aponeurosis, and ends in the basilic vein.
- A **perforator vein** connects the median cubital vein to the deep veins. It pierces the bicipital aponeurosis and fixes the median cubital vein in place.

## Lymph nodes

- The **superficial cubital (supratrochlear) nodes** lie just above the medial epicondyle, along the basilic vein. They drain the ulnar side of the hand and forearm.
- The **deep cubital node** lies at the bifurcation of the brachial artery.

Most superficial lymph vessels of the upper limb end in the axillary nodes. A few vessels from the medial side of the forearm drain into the superficial cubital nodes instead.

## Skin blood supply over the fossa

The skin of the anterolateral cubital fossa is supplied by muscle perforators from the radial collateral and radial recurrent arteries. The skin of the medial cubital fossa receives branches from an anastomosis (a joining network of arteries) between the inferior ulnar collateral and ulnar recurrent arteries. The largest fasciocutaneous perforator of the anterior forearm, the inferior cubital artery, arises from the radial artery at the lower apex of the fossa.

## Clinical anatomy (AN11.3, AN11.5)

### Venepuncture

The **median cubital vein** is the vein of choice for intravenous injections, for taking blood from donors, and for passing cardiac catheters into the heart. The reason is that the perforator vein fixes it, so it does not slip away when it is pierced.

If the median cubital vein is absent, the **basilic vein is preferred to the cephalic vein**. The basilic vein runs a straight path, whereas the cephalic vein bends acutely to drain into the axillary vein.

### Blood pressure

Blood pressure is recorded by auscultating (listening over) the **brachial artery** in front of the elbow.

### Supracondylar fracture of the humerus

The anatomy of the cubital fossa is needed to understand fractures around the elbow, especially the supracondylar fracture of the humerus.

- **Brachial artery injury** is the great danger. It was reported in more than 5% of cases before percutaneous pinning was introduced. Nowadays the incidence is probably less than 1%.
- **Nerve injury:** the median nerve is the most commonly injured nerve. The radial nerve is sometimes affected. Spontaneous recovery occurs in most cases.
- **Volkmann's ischaemia** is an ischaemic injury to the muscles and nerves of the flexor compartment of the forearm. It is caused by occlusion of the brachial artery. The muscles supplied by the anterior interosseous artery are the most susceptible, because this artery is an **end artery** (one with no collaterals joining it). The flexor pollicis longus and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus are the muscles most often affected. The ischaemia leads to compartment syndrome.

Early signs of Volkmann's ischaemia include severe forearm pain that is worse than the pain of the fracture, and inability to move the fingers fully. Other warning signs are pain on passive extension of the fingers, a tense and tender forearm, an absent pulse, blunted sensation and reduced capillary return. If these appear, urgent action is needed. The flexed elbow must be extended and all dressings removed. If the circulation does not improve, angiography or Doppler examination is done, and the vessel is repaired or grafted and a forearm fasciotomy is performed.

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Boundaries in one line:** "Lateral is the **B**rachioradialis, medial is the **P**ronator teres, base is the line between the epicondyles."
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The lateral boundary of the cubital fossa is brachioradialis. Pronator teres forms the medial boundary." %}
The lateral boundary of the cubital fossa is formed by:
- A. Biceps brachii
- B. Brachialis
- C. Brachioradialis
- D. Pronator teres
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="From medial to lateral the order is median nerve, brachial artery, tendon of biceps brachii and radial nerve (MBBR)." %}
The correct order of structures in the cubital fossa from medial to lateral is:
- A. Brachial artery, median nerve, biceps tendon, radial nerve
- B. Median nerve, brachial artery, biceps tendon, radial nerve
- C. Median nerve, biceps tendon, radial nerve, brachial artery
- D. Median nerve, brachial artery, radial nerve, biceps tendon
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="A perforator vein pierces the bicipital aponeurosis and joins the median cubital vein to the deep veins. This fixes the vein, so it does not slip away when pierced." %}
The median cubital vein is preferred for venepuncture mainly because:
- A. It lies deep to the brachial artery
- B. It is the smallest vein in the cubital region
- C. It has no communication with other veins
- D. It is fixed by a perforator vein and does not slip away
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The bicipital aponeurosis lies in front of the brachial artery at the elbow and separates it from the median cubital vein." %}
Which structure separates the brachial artery from the median cubital vein at the elbow?
- A. Bicipital aponeurosis
- B. Tendon of biceps brachii
- C. Pronator teres
- D. Brachioradialis
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Brachialis and supinator form the floor of the fossa. Brachioradialis and pronator teres form its borders, and the roof is made of skin, fascia and the bicipital aponeurosis." %}
The floor of the cubital fossa is formed by:
- A. Pronator teres and brachioradialis
- B. Brachialis and supinator
- C. Biceps brachii and brachialis
- D. Supinator and pronator teres
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The anterior interosseous artery, a branch of the brachial artery, is an end artery with no collaterals. The muscles it supplies, mainly flexor pollicis longus and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus, are the most susceptible to ischaemia after brachial artery occlusion." %}
In Volkmann's ischaemia after a supracondylar fracture, certain forearm flexor muscles are most susceptible because:
- A. They lie directly in the floor of the cubital fossa
- B. They are supplied by the radial nerve
- C. Their blood supply comes from the anterior interosseous artery, which is an end artery
- D. They are covered by the bicipital aponeurosis
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What is the cubital fossa and what is it homologous with?" %}
A triangular hollow on the front of the elbow, through which structures pass between the arm and forearm. It is homologous with the popliteal fossa on the back of the knee.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Give the boundaries of the cubital fossa." %}
The base is an imaginary line between the humeral epicondyles. The lateral border is brachioradialis and the medial border is pronator teres. The apex is where brachioradialis crosses pronator teres.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What forms the roof and floor?" %}
The roof is skin, superficial fascia (with the median cubital vein and cutaneous nerves), deep fascia and the bicipital aponeurosis. The floor is brachialis and supinator.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the contents from medial to lateral." %}
Median nerve, brachial artery (dividing into radial and ulnar arteries), tendon of biceps brachii, and the radial nerve with its deep branch.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why is the median cubital vein used for intravenous injections?" %}
A perforator vein pierces the bicipital aponeurosis and connects it to the deep veins. This fixes the vein, so it does not slip away during piercing. It is also used for taking blood from donors and for cardiac catheterisation.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which vein is preferred if the median cubital vein is absent, and why?" %}
The basilic vein, because it runs a straight path. The cephalic vein bends acutely to drain into the axillary vein.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What are the dangers of a supracondylar fracture in relation to the cubital fossa?" %}
Injury to the brachial artery, which can lead to Volkmann's ischaemia and compartment syndrome, and nerve injury, most often to the median nerve and sometimes the radial nerve.
{% /viva %}

